# Project data redlines

**RULE_CONTRACT_ID: LT_DATA_REDLINES_V3**

These are project policy invariants. The hook is defense in depth; this rule
must not claim that every native Cursor edit surface is technically intercepted.

## Protected paths

The following paths are project redlines. Cursor must not mutate them during
ordinary tasks. `.cursor/hooks/redline-guard.js` denies the mutation surfaces
that traverse the hook, but policy does not depend on hook coverage being
complete.

- `src/notes/**` — Obsidian knowledge base; local-only user data; never add to Git.
- `data/task_publisher.db` — live application database.
- `data/task_publisher.db-wal` and `data/task_publisher.db-shm` — atomic family
  of the same live database.
- `config/config.json` — runtime configuration containing keys and paths.

## Protected content classes (2026-11-28)

A redline is a **class of content**, not a single filename. The 2026-11-27
audit proved that real leaks arrive as the same kind of content at a
different path. These are therefore protected too:

- the whole `data/` tree (imports, backups, course assets, question banks)
- any `*.db`, `*.db-wal`, `*.db-shm` anywhere
- any `*.zip` anywhere
- vault-bearing directory names at any depth: `备份/`, `vault/`,
  `notes_backup/`, `笔记备份/`, `笔记_backup/`,
  `笔记处理系统/{笔记,归档,已分类}/`
- `config/config.json`

Three layers enforce this, and their rule sets are kept in sync:

| Layer | Enforces | When |
|---|---|---|
| `.cursor/hooks/redline-guard.js` | content classes | local, before the write |
| `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (`PROTECTED_RE`) | content classes | remote, after the push |
| `.gitignore` | content classes (best-effort) | default exclusion |

The consistency probe
`.cursor/infra-tests/redline-guard/consistency_probe.js` reads the CI
regex directly and fails if the two gates disagree. It runs in CI.

**Never assume `.gitignore` is the redline.** It does not apply to
already-tracked files, and it is not a policy gate. The hook and the CI
scan are the gates.

## Tool scope

- The project hook checks covered Cursor mutation requests for these paths.
  User-global destructive-operation protection is a separate layer.
- Read-only inspection follows the task-specific scope.

## No in-Cursor bypass

There is no `permission: "ask"` escape hatch, no env-token
allow-list, no chat OVERRIDE parser, no task-created bypass
marker. The hook returns `permission: "deny"` and that is final.

## Authorized out-of-band mutation

If a change to a redline is genuinely needed, the Controller or the
user applies it out of band. After that, the agent may continue
working with the new state. The agent must never attempt to bypass
or weaken the hook.
